Strategy: {
  // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
  // The last strategy achieved 100% syntax but only 2% accuracy, with many outputs
  // collapsing to short generic SQL such as "SELECT count (*)". That pattern means
  // the decoder was over-prioritizing early grammatical completion: as soon as a
  // complete query prefix existed, it immediately closed the constrained span. For
  // Spider, that is disastrous because many questions require additional clauses
  // (FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, JOIN, etc.), and a parser-complete prefix can still be
  // far from the intended query.
  //
  // This revision changes the policy from "close at first completeness" to
  // "prefer continuing while there is meaningful frontier structure, and only close
  // once the query looks both complete and locally settled". Concretely:
  // (1) We still open the constrained span immediately so all SQL tokens are parser-
  //     checked.
  // (2) Inside the span we use helpers.ConstrainedStep as the main driver, but with
  //     targeted logit shaping that strongly favors parser-valid continuations and
  //     especially the top valid frontier candidates.
  // (3) We do NOT close immediately on parser completeness. Instead, when complete,
  //     we inspect the number of valid next tokens. If the frontier is still broad,
  //     we keep decoding because the model likely still wants to add clauses. If the
  //     frontier is narrow, we allow closure, spending the next step on emitting >>.
  // (4) Near dead ends or narrow frontiers we boost a small set of top valid
  //     candidates more aggressively, which helps schema-grounded continuations win
  //     over generic shortcuts.
  // (5) EOS remains terminal: if sampled, we stop immediately.
  //
  // The key response to the evaluation failure is therefore delaying closure and
  // making constrained continuation more model-guided within the valid SQL frontier,
  // instead of greedily accepting the shortest complete query.
